.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Long simulations (a fine basin-of-attraction grid, a long Lyapunov-spectrum integration, a large parameter sweep) are worth keeping around instead of recomputing. physicskit.chaos.utils.io provides two small, generic building blocks for this: save_system_config / load_system_config round-trip a system&#x27;s class and constructor parameters through JSON (using the same __init__ introspection that already powers every system&#x27;s repr()), and save_arrays / load_arrays save any named arrays (a trajectory, a basin grid, a Poincare section) to a plain .npz archive. The system used to demonstrate this is the Lorenz attractor,">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/chaos/advanced/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_serialization_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/chaos/advanced/plot_serialization`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Saving and Loading Results</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/chaos/advanced/plot_serialization`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="physicskit.chaos.visualizers.divergence.plot_lyapunov_divergence integrates two trajectories starting an infinitesimal distance \delta_0 apart and tracks \ln(\delta_t / \delta_0) over time, where \delta_t is their separation at time t. For chaotic motion this separation grows exponentially, \delta_t \sim \delta_0 e^{\lambda_{\max} t}, so a straight, positively-sloped line (its slope the largest Lyapunov exponent \lambda_{\max} &gt; 0) is the signature of chaos; a system that is not chaotic instead shows the separation shrinking (:math:`\lambda_{\max} &lt; 0`) as both trajectories relax toward the same attracting state. Two systems are contrasted below: the chaotic Lorenz attractor, \dot{x}=\sigma(y-x), \dot{y}=x(\rho-z)-y, \dot{z}=xy-\beta z; and the Duffing oscillator, \ddot{x}+\delta_D \dot{x}+\alpha x+\beta_D x^3=\gamma\cos(\omega t), with its cubic term and forcing switched off (\beta_D=\gamma=0, \alpha=1 &gt; 0), which reduces it to a plain damped linear oscillator, \ddot{x}+\delta_D \dot{x}+x=0: every trajectory relaxes to the same fixed point, so nearby trajectories converge rather than diverge.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/chaos/chaos_metrics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_lyapunov_divergence_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/chaos/chaos_metrics/plot_lyapunov_divergence`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Lyapunov Divergence: Chaotic vs. Non-Chaotic</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/chaos/chaos_metrics/plot_lyapunov_divergence`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="While physicskit.chaos.visualizers.divergence.plot_lyapunov_divergence estimates only the largest Lyapunov exponent from a single pair of trajectories, physicskit.chaos.utils.metrics.benettin_lyapunov_spectrum estimates the full spectrum by co-evolving an orthonormal frame Y under the linearized (variational) dynamics \dot{Y} = J(x(t)) Y, where J is the system&#x27;s Jacobian, and periodically re-orthonormalizing it with a QR decomposition, accumulating the log-growth of each axis. It is applied below to the Lorenz and Rossler flows,">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/chaos/chaos_metrics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_lyapunov_spectrum_thumb.png
