    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Coloring a trajectory by a per-point scalar -- rather than drawing it in a single flat color -- can make a plot&#x27;s structure much easier to read: which parts of a billiard came early or late, or where a continuous trajectory is moving fast versus slow. Two systems are colored below: the Bunimovich stadium billiard (two straight edges joined by semicircular caps of radius r, with specular reflection \mathbf{v}&#x27; = \mathbf{v} - 2(\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{n})\,\mathbf{n} at the boundary), and the Lorenz attractor,">

